<h1>{title}</h1>

<p class="lead">Marathi has two distinct registers for "love" — <strong>प्रेम</strong> (prem, deep/committed love) and <strong>आवडते</strong> (āvaḍte, like/fancy/attraction). Using the wrong one is the #1 cultural mistake. This guide covers both, the grammar, and the phrases you will actually hear.</p>

<h2>Quick Answer</h2>
<table class="vocab-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Marathi</th><th>Literal</th><th>Register</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Romantic / lifelong partner</td><td>मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो / करते</td><td>I do love on you</td><td>Deep, committed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Parents / elders / God</td><td>मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो</td><td>I do love on you</td><td>Reverent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dating / crush / "I like you"</td><td>मला तुझी आवडते</td><td>You please me</td><td>Casual / dating</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Friend appreciation</td><td>तू माझा छान मित्र आहेस</td><td>You are my good friend</td><td>Platonic</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Grammar: Two Constructions</h2>
<h3>1. प्रेम करतो/करते — Transitive "do love"</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो</strong> (masculine speaker)</li>
  <li><strong>मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करते</strong> (feminine speaker)</li>
  <li>तुझ्यावर = on you (locative of तुझा)</li>
  <li>प्रेम = love (neuter noun)</li>
  <li>करतो/करते = do (agrees with speaker: masculine → करतो, feminine → करते)</li>
</ul>
<div class="tip-box"><strong>Tip:</strong> The verb agrees with <em>you the speaker</em>, not the object. A woman says <em>प्रेम करते</em>, a man says <em>प्रेम करतो</em>. The object (तुझ्यावर) stays the same.</div>

<h3>2. आवडते — Experiencer "pleases me"</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>मला तुझी आवडते</strong> — I like you / you please me</li>
  <li>मला = to me (dative)</li>
  <li>तुझी = your (feminine, agreeing with आवडते which treats the object as feminine)</li>
  <li>आवडते = pleases (3rd person singular, neuter/feminine agreement)</li>
</ul>
<div class="tip-box"><strong>Tip:</strong> आवडते always takes dative subject (मला, तुला, त्याला). The thing liked is the grammatical subject: <em>मला चहा आवडते</em> (tea pleases me → I like tea), <em>मला तुझी आवडते</em> (you-fem please me → I like you).</div>

<h2>Real-Life Patterns</h2>
<table class="vocab-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Marathi</th><th>Context</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Proposing / serious</td><td>मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो. तू माझी बायको/नवरा होशील का?</td><td>Marriage intent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dating / "I really like you"</td><td>मला तुझी खूप आवडते</td><td>खूप = very</td></tr>
    <tr><td>To parents</td><td>मी आई-वडिलांवर प्रेम करतो</td><td>Reverent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Breakup / "I don't love you"</td><td>मला तुझी आवडत नाही / मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करत नाही</td><td>Negation</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Cultural Etiquette</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>प्रेम is heavy.</strong> Saying मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो to someone you've dated twice is like saying "I want to marry you" on date 2. Use मला तुझी आवडते for dating.</li>
  <li><strong>Public affection is verbal, not physical.</strong> Marathi couples say प्रेम करतो in private; PDA is rare.</li>
  <li><strong>Family first.</strong> The first "I love you" a Marathi person hears is usually from parents (आई-वडिलांवर प्रेम करतो), not a partner.</li>
  <li><strong>No "love you" shortcut.</strong> English "love you" / "luv u" doesn't translate — always use the full construction.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>❌ मी तुझा प्रेम करतो</strong> → <strong>✅ मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो</strong> (locative तुझ्यावर, not तुझा)</li>
  <li><strong>❌ मला तुझा आवडते</strong> → <strong>✅ मला तुझी आवडते</strong> (feminine agreement on possessive)</li>
  <li><strong>❌ मी तुझी प्रेम करतो</strong> → <strong>✅ मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो</strong> (प्रेम takes locative, not dative)</li>
  <li><strong>❌ Using "लव यू" / "लव्ह यू"</strong> → Don't — use the Marathi forms above</li>
</ul>

<h2>Mini Practice</h2>
<ol>
  <li>"I love you (to wife/husband)" → <em>मी तुझ्यावर प्रेम करतो/करते</em></li>
  <li>"I like you (dating)" → <em>मला तुझी आवडते</em></li>
  <li>"I love my parents" → <em>मी आई-वडिलांवर प्रेम करतो</em></li>
</ol>

